words:'bowling golf skate ski volleyball stadium rank loser strength',pages:[
['To raise money for a community dinner, Jun organized a playful sports day. They could not rent the stadium, so a school allowed them to use its yard. Someone suggested bowling, another suggested golf, and two teenagers wanted to skate. Nobody wanted to ski in the middle of summer.','為了替社區晚餐募款，Jun 籌辦一場趣味運動日。他們租不起體育場，因此學校借出操場。有人提議保齡球，有人提議高爾夫，兩位青少年想溜冰。盛夏時節，沒有人想去滑雪。'],
['They finally chose volleyball and simple games. Jun refused to rank every person from best to worst. “Being called a loser does not help anyone,” he said. One boy had great strength, while another understood how to encourage a nervous teammate. Both mattered.','最後大家選了排球和簡單遊戲。Jun 不願把所有人從好到壞排名。「被叫輸家對任何人都沒有幫助，」他說。一個男孩體力強，另一人擅長鼓勵緊張的隊友；兩者都重要。'],
['The event collected enough donations for several dinners. After the final game, the group returned to the market with sore arms and a new reason to keep working together. The old recipe was starting to make sense.','活動募到足夠幾頓晚餐的捐款。最後一場比賽結束，大家帶著痠痛手臂和共同努力的新理由回到夜市。舊食譜的意思開始變清楚了。']],
